According their graphs, the one-sided limits at $x=1$ are different for both functions and therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}f(x)$ and $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}g(x)$ don't exist.

However, before we jump to any conclusions regarding $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}\Big(f(x)+g(x)\Big)$, let's consider the one-sided limits:

$\begin{align}
&\blueD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}f(x)=3}
\\\\
&\purpleD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}f(x)=1}
\\\\
&\maroonD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}g(x)=1}
\\\\
&\goldD{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}g(x)=3}
\end{align}$

We can find each one-sided limit of $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1}\Big(f(x)+g(x)\Big)$ using the fact that the limit of a sum of functions is the sum of the individual limits:

$\begin{align}
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^-}\Big(f(x)+g(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to 1^-}f(x)+\lim_{x\to 1^-}g(x)
\\\\
&\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 1^+}\Big(f(x)+g(x)\Big)=\lim_{x\to 1^+}f(x)+\lim_{x\to 1^+}g(x)
\end{align}$
